### Comparison with Xiantiandao: two Chinese salvationist traditions

The corpus now contains two distinct Chinese salvationist traditions. The comparison is analytically important:

```
XIANTIANDAO (先天道) — 先天道斋堂观音宫
  Founded: 18th-century Jiangxi/Guangdong
  Central deity: Wusheng Laomu (Eternal Venerable Mother)
  Doctrinal orientation: Buddhist-Daoist-Confucian fusion
                          + millenarian eschatology
  Institutional form: Zhaitang (vegetarian hall)
  Gender: Female-managed (斋姑)
  Public face: Guanyin devotional temple
  Syncretism: Internal/doctrinal layering (Mode 5)
  Banned: Yes, historically persecuted

DEJIAO (德教) — 德教会济芳阁
  Founded: 1939, Chaoyang, Guangdong
  Central principle: 德 (Virtue) / Five-teachings synthesis
  Doctrinal orientation: EXPLICIT five-religions universalism
                          (Daoism + Buddhism + Confucianism
                           + Christianity + Islam)
  Institutional form: 阁 (Pavilion) — moral cultivation hall
  Gender: Lay-managed, mixed
  Public face: Virtue teaching and charitable hall
  Syncretism: Explicit universalist declaration (Mode 6)
  Banned: No — publicly registered association

KEY DISTINCTION:
  Xiantiandao's syncretism is INTERNAL and CONCEALED
  (the Buddhist face hides the doctrinal depth)

  Dejiao's syncretism is EXTERNAL and DECLARED
  (the five-religions synthesis is the public teaching,
   not a concealment strategy)
```

This constitutes a sixth syncretic mode for the corpus:

```
MODE 6 — Explicit Five-Religions Universalism
          (德教会济芳阁)
          The institution publicly declares veneration of
          all five world religions' founders as equal
          pathways to virtue. Syncretism is the message,
          not an arrangement of separate traditions.
          No tradition is concealed; all are explicitly
          named. The synthesis is the institutional identity.
```

