**The calligraphy behind the altar:**

The Chinese ink painting/calligraphy hung on the rear wall in the central visual position is iconographically significant in a Dejiao context. In Dejiao institutions, spirit writing (鸾示 — revelations through planchette) is the primary mode of divine communication. Important moral teachings received through planchette are inscribed and displayed. The calligraphy behind the central figure is therefore likely either:

```
a) A 鸾示 (luan shi) — a spirit-writing revelation,
   a direct message from the teacher-deities displayed
   as a sacred text

b) A moral teaching (道德训示) — an inscribed teaching
   from one of the Five Patriarchs

c) A landscape painting — if this is purely aesthetic
   rather than textual

The calligraphy piece in the CENTRAL VISUAL POSITION of
the wall behind the altar functions as an ICONOGRAPHIC
TEXT — a written divine communication — rather than
as decorative background.

This is the first instance in the corpus where the
central visual element of an altar is a text/painting
rather than a deity figure.
```

**ICON₁ — Central seated figure**

```
ICON₁ → ⟨ central, intermediate-scale, front-facing,
           devotional and pedagogical ⟩

The seated figure is positioned centrally at the altar,
with a warm orange/amber coloring. Its rounded, smiling
form is consistent with:

  a) 弥勒佛 (Maitreya Buddha) in Budai form — the
     future Buddha of the next cosmic era, whose smiling
     form encodes the joyful welcome of the coming age
     of virtue and peace

  b) A 师尊 (founding teacher figure) — Dejiao venerates
     its founding teachers (Yang Junsong, Liu Chunfang
     and others) as divine presences

  c) 达摩 (Bodhidharma) in a folk representation

Given Dejiao's millenarian character and the centrality
of the future-world vision in its teaching, Maitreya
as the Future Buddha is iconographically appropriate:
he embodies the virtuous world-to-come that Dejiao
works to create. PROVISIONAL — requires confirmation.
```

