### The Five Patriarchs and iconographic absence

The most significant iconographic feature of 德教会济芳阁 is what is **not present**: the elaborate deity-figure assemblies that characterize every other institution in this corpus.

```
CORPUS-WIDE DEITY DENSITY COMPARISON:

Loyang Tua Pek Kong:    Many altars, dozens of deities
                        across four traditions
Soon Thian Keing:       Three-bay main hall + back hall;
                        8+ deities in right shrine alone
Mun San Fook Tuck Chee: Three-bay; 金花夫人 + 12 attendants
                        + 12 maidens + 5 direction deities
Kwan Im Thong Hood Cho: Guanyin + Sakyamuni + Bodhidharma
                        + Hua Tuo + Tian Gong
LSSL:                   27 numbered structures across
                        the entire compound

德教会济芳阁:            One central figure. One
                        calligraphy scroll. Two pillar
                        inscriptions.
```

The iconographic sparseness is doctrinally motivated, not incidental. Dejiao's Five Patriarchs doctrine creates a specific challenge for figural representation:

```
Laozi:     Can be represented as a deity figure
Sakyamuni: Can be represented as a Buddha figure
Confucius: Can be represented as a scholar figure
Jesus:     Christianity generally discourages idol
           worship (within Chinese Christianity,
           icons are used but not deity statues)
Muhammad:  Islam strictly prohibits any figural
           representation of the Prophet

Therefore: A fully figural representation of all Five
           Patriarchs as deity statues is impossible
           within Dejiao's own theological commitments.

This is the FIRST institution in the corpus where
the iconographic programme is constrained by the
ANTI-FIGURAL commitments of one of its own integrated
traditions (Islam).

Solution: Dejiao represents the Five Patriarchs
          primarily through text (calligraphy, spirit-
          writing revelations) and through the single
          accessible central figure (likely Maitreya or
          founder-teacher), while the Islamic and
          Christian presences are encoded textually
          rather than figurally.
```

This creates a new RFIG observation about the relationship between iconographic programme and doctrinal commitment:

```
When a multi-religious institution integrates traditions
that carry anti-figural commitments, the iconographic
programme necessarily becomes TEXTUAL rather than
FIGURAL for those components.

The calligraphy scroll in the central visual position
may therefore be performing the work that deity figures
perform in other corpus institutions: encoding the
presence of the divine in the space. The difference is
that the presence is encoded through WORD rather than
through IMAGE.

ENSEMBLE →
  [SPIRIT_WRITING_TEXT  substitutes  DEITY_FIGURES]
    [scope: shrine]
  [at: Dejiao institutions where anti-figural
       traditions are integrated]
```

